            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Conclusion First: Compliance Audits Are 'Preventive Medicine,' Not 'Emergency Care'</h2>
<p>Many overseas teams only start caring about compliance when their accounts get banned, ads get rejected, or balances get frozen — but by then, it's already too late. <strong>Compliance audits are routine 'health checks,' not post-incident damage control.</strong> A standardized audit SOP can help cross-border teams avoid pitfalls and stop wasting money when platform regulations tighten.</p>
<p>Based on our work with cross-border e-commerce, gaming, and SaaS clients, over 70% of account bans could have been detected and fixed during routine audits — but teams usually lack systematic checking processes.</p>
<h2>Checkpoint 1: Account Basic Status Audit (Daily/Weekly Must-Do)</h2>
<p>This is the most overlooked yet critical step. Account status checks aren't just about balance — watch for these signals:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Payment method health</strong>: Is the credit card prepaid balance sufficient? Avoid triggering risk controls due to payment failures</li>
<li><strong>Account permission configuration</strong>: Are BM admin, account admin, and ad manager permissions clearly defined? Are departing employees' accounts removed promptly?</li>
<li><strong>Ad account association risks</strong>: Do multiple accounts under the same entity have compliance-related associations? One banned account can affect others</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: Build an Account Status Checklist and spend 10 minutes checking key indicators every morning before launching campaigns.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Checkpoint 2: Ad Creative &amp; Landing Page Compliance (Weekly Core Check)</h2>
<p>Creative rejections and landing page violations are the most common 'minefields' in overseas advertising. Platform review rules are numerous and frequently updated — teams must establish proactive inspection mechanisms.</p>
<p><strong>Text creative checklist:</strong><br />
- Avoid exaggerated promotional words (e.g., 'best,' 'first,' 'guaranteed returns')<br />
- Price promotions must include original prices and discount percentages<br />
- Privacy policy links must be valid</p>
<p><strong>Visual creative checklist:</strong><br />
- Portrait authorization certificates must be complete<br />
- Competitor logos and trademarks must not appear<br />
- Zero tolerance for violence and discriminatory content</p>
<p><strong>Landing page compliance:</strong><br />
- Payment process must have security certification (HTTPS required)<br />
- Return/exchange policies and privacy terms must be fully displayed<br />
- Page content must match ad promises (avoid exaggerated landing pages)</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: A tool app client was repeatedly rejected by Meta due to a missing privacy policy link on their landing page. After adding the link, their ad approval rate jumped from 45% to 82%.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Checkpoint 3: Financial Reconciliation &amp; Spend Anomaly Monitoring (Daily Must-Do)</h2>
<p>Compliance issues often come with financial anomalies. When accounts face abnormal spending, fake traffic, or get flagged as 'high risk,' financial data will 'sound the alarm' first.</p>
<p><strong>Financial audit core metrics:</strong><br />
- Day-over-day spend increase without reasonable cause (&gt;30%)<br />
- Cost per acquisition (CPA) abnormal fluctuations<br />
- Sudden rise in refund rates or cart abandonment rates</p>
<p><strong>Anomaly handling SOP:</strong><br />
1. Detect anomaly → 2. Screenshot and preserve evidence → 3. Contact platform support to appeal → 4. Pause relevant ad sets → 5. Root cause review</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: Some teams have inadequate balance warning settings, causing forced suspension when accounts go into arrears. Set alerts when balance drops below 20% and configure auto-reload or switch to backup accounts.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Final Thoughts: Compliance Is a Cost — and a Competitive Advantage</h2>
<p>Many SMB overseas teams view compliance investment as 'waste' — if you don't violate rules, isn't that enough? In reality, <strong>one account ban (frozen balance, lost spend history, re-onboarding costs) often costs 5-10 times more than prevention.</strong></p>
<p>The core value of a compliance audit SOP isn't 'not getting into trouble' but <strong>building a reproducible risk control mechanism</strong> so teams can scale without anxiety about ad bans and account shutdowns.</p>
<p>If you're managing multiple overseas ad accounts, start implementing your first audit checklist today.</p>
